==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|capricieux}}, from {{der|en|it|capriccioso}}, from {{m|it|capriccio}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈpɹɪʃəs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-capricious.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-capricious.oga|a=US}}
* {{rhyme|en|ɪʃəs|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[impulsive|Impulsive]] and [[unpredictable]]; [[determine]]d by [[chance#Noun|chance]], [[impulse]], or [[whim]].
#: {{synonyms|en|arbitrary|whimsical|fickle|flighty|wayward}}
#: {{antonyms|en|conscientious|rigorous}}
#: {{ux|en|I almost died in a '''capricious''' winter storm.}}
#: {{ux|en|Stringent rulers are unlikely to act '''capriciously'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|page=198|scene=iii|act=III|passage=I am heere with thee, and thy Goats, as the moſt '''capricious''' Poet honeſt ''Ouid'' was among Gothes.}}
#* {{RQ:James American|page=11|passage="'''Capricious'''?" And at this monsieur began to laugh. "Oh no, I'm not '''capricious'''. I am very faithful. I am very constant. ''Comprenez''?"}}

====Usage notes====
* The word ''capricious'' can describe both a person and the decisions which they make.

